cartridge "QUOLERUS/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1988"

' QUOLERUS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Quolerus", COMPUTE!'s Gazette, November
' 1988 (Commodore 64, a BASIC program QUOLERUS with machine language
' QUOLERUS.ML at $C000 and sprites QUOLERUS.SPR at $3000; no author named
' in the listing). The listing notes that its game is based on a 1981
' Atari game, used with permission; this version uses none of that game's
' names, characters or pictures. Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made
' by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' BASIC and machine code, orig/quolerus-ml.dis, and timed in jsc64), the
' game is built for the ColecoVision: a wall of bevelled coloured cells,
' a dart-shaped ship in eight headings, a one-eyed pod, spinning orbs,
' smooth sprites and sound effects, an original title tune.
'
' The game: a pod hides behind a wall of cells on the left and rides up
' and down. Every time it turns it may let out an orb (up to the level's
' number); orbs bounce all over the screen and, when one lines up with
' your ship across or down, it fires a missile straight at you. Turn your
' ship, thrust, and shoot: each shot knocks one cell out of the wall. The
' wall's columns keep rolling, the even ones up and the odd ones down, so
' the holes move. Touching the wall, the pod, an orb or a missile costs a
' ship. Shoot the pod through a gap to clear the level.
'
' From the original: the pod at the far left riding between the top and
' the bottom (1.5 pixels a frame here, the C64's about 2.5 of its larger
' pixels), turning and letting out an orb at the turn when a slot is free
' and the turn is not the same way as the last orb's; an orb leaves up and
' left when the pod turns up, down and right when it turns down, moves
' diagonally and bounces off the field's edges, flies over the wall, and
' fires a missile (one at a time) along a row or a column when it lines
' up with the ship; missiles stop at the wall; the ship turns through
' eight headings (about 10 a second while held), thrusts in the heading
' and coasts to a stop over about 24 pixels when the stick is let go,
' still in the heading of its last thrust; one shot at a time, held
' button fires again; the wall's columns roll (wrapping round), the even
' ones up and the odd ones down, and shot cells stay shot when a ship is
' lost; the levels: wall 4 columns and 1-4 orbs (levels 1-4), 6 columns
' and 2-4 orbs (5-7), 8 columns and 2-4 orbs (8-10, then 8-10 again and
' again); at 3 or 4 orbs the top orb slot is retired after 5 of its orbs
' are shot, then the next one down (never the first two); scoring: a cell
' 50, an orb 100, a missile 50, the pod 2100 (the C64 gave 2000 + 100),
' an extra ship every 10000; three ships to start.
'
' Liberties: the field is 28 x 21 tiles (the C64's 40 x 25) with the
' wall in columns 9-16; the wall rolls every 6 frames (the C64 every 4
' jiffies) and the speeds are slowed for a human player (see above; the
' missile 1.5 pixels a frame like the ship, the shot 4, the C64's shot
' as fast as the ship); collisions are box tests (the
' C64 used its sprite collision registers); an orb's missile is aimed
' when the orb is within one pixel of the ship's row or column (the C64
' needed an exact match); an orb's kills count when it is hit (the C64
' counted when its burst ended); pulling the stick down brakes a coasting
' ship (the C64 had no brake); a ship lost or the pod shot freezes the
' field while the burst plays, as on the C64; the explosion art and all
' sounds are new; GAME OVER, then the title (the C64 showed its title
' with the score).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): left and right turn the ship, up
' thrusts, down brakes, either button fires. On the title, up and down
' choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects
' after; the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always
' given.

